\section{Introduction}
Reward learning is a critical yet difficult task, particularly when rewards cannot be directly observed. In many scenarios, rewards are only partially visible, requiring supplementation with extrinsic rewards to fully represent real-world objectives. This is especially relevant in service robotics, where environmental conditions are often unpredictable and rewards must be learned from partial observations to guide robots toward goal-oriented actions. Additionally, in multi-objective scenarios, rewards must be acquired from multiple sources, such as multiple operators, to address complex tasks.

