\section{Introduction}
The inter-beat interval (IBI) is a critical metric in physiological monitoring, defined as the time span between consecutive heartbeats. Although R-R intervals are the standard measure for IBI in electrocardiogram (ECG) studies, this work presents a method to estimate IBIs from single-lead ECGs that is inherently robust against noise. This capability is particularly valuable for applications such as fetal heart rate monitoring, where uterine contractions introduce significant interference.

In standard ECG analysis, R-R intervals are calculated by detecting QRS complexes and measuring the time between successive R peaks. However, this method is highly sensitive to noise because QRS complexes can be obscured by interference. Conversely, our proposed IBI estimation technique is resilient to noise because it does not depend on individual R peaks. Instead, it treats IBIs as a sequential time series, utilizing machine learning to model the underlying physiological dynamics.

